            "lens": "review-reliability",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R3-001",
                "lens": "reliability",
                "location": "engine/skills/approval_digest.go:1",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "The domain behavior this change introduces has no test in the candidate. That covers SkillDigest (ordering, exclusion of the root approval record but not a nested file of that name, refusal of links, pipes and line-break names, and the combined error message) and ClassifyApproval (a version 1 record classified as stale, an unverifiable skill, digest mismatch after a file is added or removed). The only new tests are in skillsfs and cover the store's listing of entries. The change also deletes the grandfathering baseline and its tests, and no replacement tests for the gate (EvaluateApproval, CheckApprovals) appear.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/skills/approval_digest.go:1",
                  "engine/skills/approval.go:223",
                  "engine/skills/approval_gate.go:120"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "insufficient",
                "causal_disposition": "introduced"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 10 changed paths in full.",
              "The store reads without following links or opening pipes. A read error on any file or subdirectory fails the whole listing, so no file is silently dropped from the digest. The root approval record is listed but not read. All of this is exercised by the skillsfs tests, including the FIFO test, which has a timeout.",
              "ClassifyApproval and CheckApprovals handle the paths I traced correctly: a legacy v1 record is stale, a link or pipe makes the skill unverifiable, a missing SKILL.md is set aside, and the digest used for classification comes from the same read.",
              "The Approvals store tests are externally observable and sit at the cheapest level. The gap is limited to the domain-level digest and classification tests, which are not in the visible candidate."
            ]
          }
        }
